  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Paolo Vitelli was the Italian entrepreneur who founded Azimut and built the Azimut-Benetti yacht group. Born in Turin and trained in economics, he used proceeds from a student nightclub venture to enter boating. He expanded from charter and imports into manufacturing, acquiring Benetti and developing an international production and sales network.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Primary company history and industry obituary establish the commercial career and early earned financing."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Paolo Vitelli was the Italian entrepreneur who founded Azimut and built the Azimut-Benetti yacht group. Born in Turin and trained in economics, he used proceeds from a student nightclub venture to enter boating. He expanded from charter and imports into manufacturing, acquiring Benetti and developing an international production and sales network.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Shipbuilder Paolo Vitelli entered Alpine hospitality after a property plan changed direction. He and his partners intended to convert Champoluc's old Breithorn hotel into apartments, but the mayor required it to remain a hotel. Vitelli continued alone, restoring it for guests.\n\nThe nearby village of Mascognaz offered a different project, rooted in childhood memories of the mountains. He preserved historic Walser chalets as a hotel spread through the settlement. During restoration, stones and beams were taken apart, cleaned, numbered and returned to their original positions. The village's former school became the first hotel building.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Firsthand historical interview and later reporting distinguish the original restoration from developments undertaken after his death."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S5",
      "S6",
      "S7"
    ],
    "word_count": 94,
    "html": "<p>Shipbuilder Paolo Vitelli entered Alpine hospitality after a property plan changed direction. He and his partners intended to convert Champoluc's old Breithorn hotel into apartments, but the mayor required it to remain a hotel. Vitelli continued alone, restoring it for guests.</p>\r\n<p>The nearby village of Mascognaz offered a different project, rooted in childhood memories of the mountains. He preserved historic Walser chalets as a hotel spread through the settlement. During restoration, stones and beams were taken apart, cleaned, numbered and returned to their original positions. The village's former school became the first hotel building.</p>\r\n"
  },
